course: dictionary
lesson: 13537
title: Polite and impolite
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Impolite means not polite. It describes behaviour that shows bad manners or a lack of respect for other people. The prefix im- means "not", so impolite is the opposite of polite.
explain: Clues in a sentence can show you what impolite means. «Sam kept interrupting his grandma while she told her story, and he never said thank you.» Interrupting and forgetting to say thank you are not kind to other people, so impolite must mean rude.
explain: Test your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «It is impolite to stare at people.» If you put "rude" in place of impolite and the sentence still makes sense, you have found the right meaning.
fact: Impolite means not polite: rude, and not showing good manners or respect to others.
fact: The prefix im- means "not", so impolite is the opposite of polite.
fact: «It is impolite to interrupt» someone who is speaking.
fact: Impolite things include staring, talking with your mouth full, and ignoring someone who speaks to you.
fact: In stories, an impolite character often learns a lesson about respect.
exercise: «It is impolite to interrupt when someone else is speaking.» What does impolite mean? | Very quiet | Happy and excited | *Rude, not showing good manners | Kind and helpful
exercise: Which of these is impolite? | Waiting your turn to speak | *Talking with your mouth full at the table | Saying please when you ask for something | Saying thank you after a gift
exercise: Find and copy one word from this sentence that means not polite: «Ben ignored his friend when she said hello, which was impolite.» | ignored | friend | hello | *impolite
test: Which word means the opposite of polite? | *Impolite | Kind | Quiet | Gentle
test: «Staring at someone for a long time is impolite.» In this sentence, impolite means | Friendly | Very interesting | *Rude and not respectful | Sleepy
exam: Which sentence shows impolite behaviour? | Saying please and thank you at dinner | Holding the door open for a visitor | Helping a neighbour carry the shopping | *Shouting over your friend every time she tries to speak
